WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
In this lesson, we're looking at plan mode in Cloud Code. Plan mode separates the thinking

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.560
phase from execution. Before Cloud touches a single file, it produces a structured proposal

3
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.399
you can review and approve if you use plan mode. We'll cover when plan mode earns its place,

4
00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:26.079
how to trigger it, and what to look for before you say yes. Let's go. Plan mode is a read-only

5
00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:30.959
thinking phase. Cloud inspects your code base, reasons about what needs to happen,

6
00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:36.479
and hands you an ordered proposal without writing a single file. The approval gate is the checkpoint

7
00:00:36.479 --> 00:00:42.560
where you decide whether that proposal is correct. You can accept it, edit it, or reject it outright,

8
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.119
and no file writes happen until you pass through. Re-planning is what happens when partial execution

9
00:00:49.119 --> 00:00:55.439
exposes something the original proposal got wrong. Cloud regenerates or amends the plan before

10
00:00:55.439 --> 00:01:00.479
continuing rather than improvising around the problem. Plan mode, the approval gate,

11
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:05.440
and re-planning together form the backbone of a controlled, reversible workflow.

12
00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:11.919
So what does plan mode actually do under the hood? Cloud enters a read-only thinking phase.

13
00:01:12.559 --> 00:01:18.080
It explores the code base, reading the files it thinks are relevant, reasons through the task,

14
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.599
and produces a structured proposal. That proposal isn't a commitment. It is a draft

15
00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:29.440
Cloud is handing to you for review. The proposal includes the steps it plans to take, the files it

16
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.519
expects to read or write, and any risks or assumptions it is making explicit. What it

17
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:42.080
doesn't do is touch any of those files. The moment you approve the plan, Cloud exits read-only mode

18
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.639
and starts executing. That transition is the key thing to internalize. Thinking and doing

19
00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:55.440
are kept separate intentionally. There are two ways to enter plan mode, and both are deliberate.

20
00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.080
The first is the shift plus tab toggle. Plan mode is a permission mode, and shift plus tab cycles

21
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:08.559
through them. Default, then accept edits, then plan. When you land on plan, the whole session

22
00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:13.600
stays in plan mode until you cycle back out. That's what you want when a longer piece of work

23
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:20.080
should stay in review first mode. The second way is the slash plan prefix. You put slash plan in

24
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.679
front of a single request, and Cloud plans just that one prompt before returning to whatever mode

25
00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:31.279
the session was in. Use the toggle when you want to stay in plan mode. Use the prefix when you want

26
00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:37.520
one plan without flipping the session. Here's a framing that clears up a lot of confusion.

27
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.279
Plan mode isn't a separate tool you launch. It's one of the permission modes the session can be in.

28
00:02:43.279 --> 00:02:48.960
Right alongside the default mode and accept edits mode. That's why both entry paths we just covered

29
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.320
are really just ways of setting the permission mode. Shift plus tab cycles through the modes

30
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.759
interactively until you land on plan. If you'd rather start a session already in plan mode,

31
00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:05.839
you pass permission mode plan on the command line when you launch Cloud. Once you're in plan mode

32
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:11.520
and Cloud hands you a proposal, you have three choices. You can approve it, you can edit it before

33
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.199
approving, or you can reject it and ask for a different approach. Approving is the specific

34
00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:23.279
action that flips the session out of plan mode and lets Cloud start making changes. What does a

35
00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:28.639
well-formed plan actually look like? There are three components you should expect every time.

36
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:34.399
First, ordered steps. The plan should be a numbered sequence of discrete actions, not a paragraph of

37
00:03:34.399 --> 00:03:41.039
intentions. Second, files touched. Cloud should name every file it expects to read or write.

38
00:03:41.039 --> 00:03:47.839
If a file is missing from this list, ask for it explicitly before approving. Third, risks called

39
00:03:47.839 --> 00:03:53.600
out. A good plan flags its own assumptions. Things like, I'm assuming this interface isn't used

40
00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.639
elsewhere, or this depends on the migration having run. If any are missing, push back and ask Cloud

41
00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:06.080
to fill in the gaps. That back and forth is iterative refinement and it costs far less

42
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:13.679
than fixing code afterward. When does plan mode actually earn its keep? Cross-file refactors are

43
00:04:13.679 --> 00:04:20.079
the clearest case. If you're renaming a type used across dozens of files, a plan surfaces every

44
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:26.720
touchpoint before execution starts. Schema changes are equally risky. Modifying a database schema

45
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.359
or an API contract ripples through models, validators, and tests, and a plan lets you verify

46
00:04:33.359 --> 00:04:39.200
that all affected layers are accounted for. Ambiguous requests are the third case. When the

47
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.880
right approach is genuinely unclear, plan mode externalizes Cloud's interpretation so you can

48
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.959
correct it before any damage is done. And on complex work, plan mode pairing helps. Plan

49
00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:56.720
mode combined with a sub-agent that investigates first, or a hook that validates steps before they

50
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.679
run, raises reliability further. Plan mode isn't the right tool for every task. There are cases

51
00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:08.640
where the overhead of planning adds more friction than it prevents. A single file change with a

52
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:15.519
clear mechanical fix doesn't need a plan. If you're renaming one variable or correcting a typo, just

53
00:05:15.519 --> 00:05:22.160
do it. Exploratory work in a REPL, where reversibility is trivially easy, doesn't benefit from a formal

54
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.720
proposal either. The useful heuristic is use plan mode when the work is hard to reverse or when it

55
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:35.040
spans multiple modules. If you can undo a mistake in 10 seconds, the approval gate isn't adding much.

56
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:41.440
If a mistake would take 30 minutes to unwind, the plan is worth it. Before you approve any plan,

57
00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.559
you need to read it critically. This sounds obvious, but approving without reading defeats

58
00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.399
the whole purpose. There are three things to check. First, does the scope match your intent?

59
00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:58.480
Claude sometimes interprets a request more broadly than you meant, and a plan that touches 10 files

60
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:05.040
when you expected two is a signal to narrow the task before proceeding. Second, are the file paths

61
00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.160
correct for your actual project layout? Claude's assumptions about directory structure aren't

62
00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.799
always accurate. Third, does the plan flag risks you can already resolve from domain knowledge?

63
00:06:16.799 --> 00:06:22.399
Catching a wrong assumption at the review stage costs seconds. Catching it after half the files

64
00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:30.079
are rewritten costs much more. People treat plan mode like a politeness setting, a way of asking

65
00:06:30.079 --> 00:06:35.920
Claude to explain itself before diving in. It really isn't that. It's a permission boundary,

66
00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.959
and nothing gets written to disk until you approve the plan.

67
00:06:39.679 --> 00:06:44.880
That difference is why the proposal is worth reading carefully. If it's just a courtesy,

68
00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.640
skimming costs you nothing. If it's the gate, careful review is the whole point.

69
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.239
That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next video.

